From fcf86eff0f8a9a7c42f36e168417557285a9adb5
Subject: bevy_tasks: multi-threaded TaskPool on the web under the web-worker feature
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/fcf86eff0f8a9a7c42f36e168417557285a9adb5
Date: 2026-09-22T23:04:15+01:00

With atomics and shared memory the web can run real threads, but the pool
was still gated to the single-threaded implementation on every wasm
target, so nothing bevy parallelises could use them. Select the
multi-threaded pool under the new `web-worker` feature, which needs the
atomics target feature and fails the build without it. The feature
rather than atomics alone, because a scope parks the thread that opens
it, which the browser forbids on the main thread: the feature is what
says the app runs on a worker. With no
workers joined the pool degrades to the single-threaded behaviour, so
there is no reason to leave it off once the app is on a worker.

The pool spawns no threads on the web: a worker needs the module's JS
glue to instantiate over the shared memory, which bevy_tasks has no
access to. Whoever has it (the page, via `bevy::web_worker`) spawns the
workers, and the pool instead exposes `run_worker`, which a worker calls
once after initialising its instance to become one of the pool's threads
until the pool is dropped. The pool works before
any thread has joined, and threads may never join, so a scope's thread
always works the pool too and counts towards `thread_num` for batching.

`spawn` keeps the single-threaded pool's contract on the web: a static,
non-Send future driven by the JS event loop. A pool thread never returns
to its event loop, so a future spawned from one is handed to the thread
the pool was created on and driven there. Like the single-threaded pool,
such futures must not capture anything bound to the spawning thread.
`Task::split` separates the task handle from the driving future so the
caller chooses where it runs.

bevy_asset's processor scoped `process_asset` futures on the IoTaskPool;
they are not Send on the web, so process the reprocess queue inline there
instead. The processor is not used on the web.

The pipelined renderer's extract no longer needs its web-only `block_on`
on the render-app channel: the scope's thread works this pool and can
wait, so the upstream `scope_with_executor` path runs on the engine
worker too.
